# Build-safe edits

Code changes must not introduce build or runtime errors. Follow these every time you edit.

## JSX / TSX

- **Never use escaped double quotes inside a double-quoted JSX attribute.** The parser can treat `\"` as an invalid escape and fail the build.
  - **Bad:** `message="Try \"Leave feedback\" again."`
  - **Good:** `message={\`Try "Leave feedback" again.\`}` or `message='Try "Leave feedback" again.'`
- Prefer template literals in `{}` when the string contains quotes or apostrophes.

## Before finishing

- After editing `.tsx`/`.ts`/`.jsx`/`.js`, run `npm run build` (or suggest the user run it) so broken syntax or type errors are caught before push. Do not rely only on the linter.
- If you change coach application signup or `lib/coach-application-signup.ts`, run `npm test` so insert-column drift tests pass.
- If the project uses Next.js, the build uses SWC; avoid ambiguous escapes and non-standard JS in JSX.
